The deliberation has converged on the key trade-offs between standing pat and drawing to a straight. Colleagues 7 and 10 have retracted their support for keeping {3, 3, 3}, recognizing that rerolling two dice yields an expected value of 18.19 points, strictly inferior to the 20.00 points guaranteed by standing pat on {3, 3, 3, 5, 6}. The remaining disagreement centers on whether the prompt assumes an open scorecard allowing Chance to absorb missed straights or requires completing a specific category combination. Under the standard single-turn expected value objective where each terminal roll scores in its highest legally available open box, keeping {3, 5, 6} maximizes the expected score at 24.17 points due to straight equity (11/36) and a strong fallback sum averaging 20.80 points on misses. Therefore, keeping 3, 5, and 6 remains the mathematically optimal strategy.
}

